<h2>The case in one view</h2>
<table class="law">
  <tr><th>Case</th><td>Sayed Khalid Ali v. Arshi, Criminal Appeal No. 78/2025</td></tr>
  <tr><th>Court</th><td>Ms. Shilpi Jain, Additional Sessions Judge-02, Central District, Tis Hazari Courts, Delhi</td></tr>
  <tr><th>Decided</th><td>2 November 2026</td></tr>
  <tr><th>Provision</th><td>Appeal under Section 29, Protection of Women from Domestic Violence Act, 2005</td></tr>
  <tr><th>Outcome</th><td>Appeal dismissed; Magistrate&rsquo;s order of 7 February 2025 upheld</td></tr>
</table>

<h2>The background</h2>
<p>The parties were husband and wife who lived in a one-room rented house in Darya Ganj, Delhi. On 8 July 2023, the wife left for her parental home, and thereafter filed a complaint under Section 12 of the DV Act. On 28 August 2023, the Magistrate fixed interim maintenance of Rs 3,000 per month in her favour. A related FIR under Sections 498A, 406 and 34 of the Indian Penal Code also existed between the parties.</p>
<p>The husband then moved an application in the wife&rsquo;s own DV proceedings, seeking a direction that she not obstruct his re-entry into the rented premises. He alleged that her family members had forcibly entered the house on 3 September 2023. The Magistrate dismissed that application on 7 February 2025, and the husband carried the matter to the Sessions Court under Section 29 of the Act.</p>

<h2>Limitation: the Sunday rule</h2>
<p>Before the merits, the court addressed limitation. The thirtieth day from the impugned order fell on a Sunday. Applying Section 4 of the Limitation Act, 1963 and Section 10 of the General Clauses Act, 1897, the court held the appeal filed on the next working day to be within time — a small but recurring point of practice in DV Act appeals, where the thirty-day window under Section 29 is short and often ends on a court holiday.</p>

<h2>The right to reside does not lapse with absence</h2>
<p>The substantive foundation of the decision is Section 17 of the DV Act. Relying on the Supreme Court&rsquo;s decisions in <em>Satish Chander Ahuja</em> and <em>Prabha Tyagi</em>, the court reiterated that every woman in a domestic relationship has the right to reside in the shared household, and that this right does not depend on her actually residing there at any given moment. The wife&rsquo;s departure to her parental home in July 2023 therefore did not dilute her statutory right in respect of the rented Darya Ganj premises.</p>

<h2>No reliefs for the respondent in Section 12 proceedings</h2>
<p>The appeal failed primarily on the architecture of the statute. The reliefs under the DV Act — protection orders under Section 18, residence orders under Section 19, monetary relief under Section 20, custody orders under Section 21 and compensation under Section 22 — are all framed as reliefs for the aggrieved person. The court put the point directly:</p>
<div class="note">
<p><strong>From the judgment:</strong> &ldquo;The DV Act does not contemplate the grant of reliefs in favour of the respondent in the said proceedings.&rdquo;</p>
</div>
<p>The court further held that the Magistrate&rsquo;s refusal was in no way perverse, because a direction permitting the husband to reside in the premises would, in effect, operate to exclude the wife from the shared household — inverting the protective scheme of the Act. The disputed facts surrounding the alleged forcible entry of 3 September 2023 were left to be examined at trial.</p>

<h2>What the decision means in practice</h2>
<div class="tiles">
  <div class="tile"><strong>For respondents.</strong> A husband or in-law arrayed as a respondent cannot convert the aggrieved person&rsquo;s Section 12 case into a forum for his own reliefs. Claims to possession or entry must be pursued in independent civil proceedings.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>For aggrieved persons.</strong> Leaving the shared household — whether for safety or family support — does not surrender the Section 17 right to reside. Interim orders protecting that right survive the absence.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>For appellate strategy.</strong> Section 29 appeals are tested on perversity and legal error, not on a fresh weighing of disputed facts. Where facts are genuinely contested, the Sessions Court will ordinarily leave them to trial.</div>
</div>

<h2>The wider frame</h2>
<p>The decision sits within a settled line of authority on the one-directional character of DV Act reliefs. The statute was enacted to secure the civil protection of women facing domestic violence, and the definition of &ldquo;aggrieved person&rdquo; in Section 2(a) confines the claimant class to women in a domestic relationship. Respondents retain their ordinary remedies — civil suits for possession or injunction, and defences within the DV proceedings themselves — but they cannot obtain affirmative orders under Sections 18 to 22. For practitioners in Delhi&rsquo;s district courts, the judgment is a clean, citable statement of that proposition at the appellate level, together with a useful application of the limitation rules for Section 29 appeals.</p>
